+ * The kernel keeps one written bit per page, and two cache entries can cover
+ * the same page (one buffer read in two formats), so a reset for one entry
+ * must not hide the write from the other. Each query therefore moves the bits
+ * it resets into a per-page epoch here; an entry has changed if any of its
+ * pages has an epoch newer than the entry's last hash. A write landing during
+ * or after a hash gets a newer epoch at the next query. Small spans, other
+ * platforms and a failed query all fall back to hashing. */
+#define ENG_WATCH_MIN_SPAN (64u * 1024u)
